  "status_text": "Tonight's three checked rules about shrinking a graph are now told in one article: keep part of a graph, drop a duplicate row, or clean away a wire nobody reads, and the cheapest circuit never gets bigger. The third, checked in Lean on one-a-side inputs: take a fan-in-two AND/OR/NOT circuit that is right on a graph; if no gate the output reaches lists some input wire, and that wire is not the output, then some such circuit with at most as many gates is right on the same graph and lists that wire nowhere [L-meta-25001].",
